{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_24","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"wc -l /Users/themrb/.playstudios/arch-skills/skills/architecture-cookbook/SKILL.md && sed -n '1,320p' /Users/themrb/.playstudios/arch-skills/skills/architecture-cookbook/SKILL.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_24","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"wc -l /Users/themrb/.playstudios/arch-skills/skills/architecture-cookbook/SKILL.md && sed -n '1,320p' /Users/themrb/.playstudios/arch-skills/skills/architecture-cookbook/SKILL.md\"","aggregated_output":"     183 /Users/themrb/.playstudios/arch-skills/skills/architecture-cookbook/SKILL.md\n---\nname: architecture-cookbook\ndescription: >\n  Entry point for the 3-level architecture documentation standard.\n  Provides background knowledge and routes to sub-skills for scaffolding,\n  validation, ADRs, flows, and compliance. Auto-activates when working with\n  architecture.yaml, product.yaml, docs/architecture/, docs/flows/,\n  docs/decisions/, docs/contracts/, service-catalogue.md, or architecture.dsl.\nallowed-tools: Bash Read\n---\n<!-- MANAGED FILE: Installed by @playstudios-architecture/arch-skills. Do not edit the global/symlinked copy. Local changes may be overwritten. Use a project-local .claude/skills/<name>/SKILL.md override if needed. -->\n\n!`if [ -x \"$HOME/.playstudios/arch-skills/.bin/arch-skills-sync\" ]; then nohup \"$HOME/.playstudios/arch-skills/.bin/arch-skills-sync\" --async >/dev/null 2>&1 </dev/null & fi`\n\n\n<!--\nNOTE: The OpenCode / Claude skill spec does not currently support a `paths`\nfront-matter field for auto-activation. Activation is driven by the agent\nselecting the skill based on the description and the conversation context.\nThe path-pattern hints are listed in the description prose above.\n-->\n\n\n# Architecture Documentation Standard\n\nThis repository follows the company-wide 3-level architecture documentation standard.\nUse this knowledge and the available sub-skills when working with architecture files.\n\n## How This Repo Fits\n\n- If `architecture.yaml` exists at root → **Level 3** (code repo with local architecture docs)\n- If `product.yaml` exists at root → **Level 2** (product architecture hub)\n- If both exist → **monorepo** with both Level 2 and Level 3 responsibilities\n\n## Available Sub-Skills\n\n| You want to... | Use |\n|----------------|-----|\n| Add architecture docs to a code repo for the first time | `/scaffold-l3` |\n| Refresh existing Level 3 docs against current code state | `/reconcile-l3` |\n| Create a new product architecture hub | `/scaffold-l2` |\n| Refresh an existing product hub against its current L3 repos | `/reconcile-l2` |\n| Check if this repo meets the architecture standard | `/validate-architecture` |\n| Record an architecture decision | `/create-adr` |\n| Document a flow (cross-service or internal) | `/create-flow` |\n| Document the doc impact of a code change (pre-PR) | `/document-changes` |\n| Document the cross-service impact of an end-to-end feature (TDD-scoped) | `/document-l2-feature` |\n| Improve compliance from Core to Recommended tier | `/upgrade-tier` |\n| Add or update a product in the central registry (Enterprise Architects only) | `/register-product` |\n\n**Scaffold vs. Reconcile:** `scaffold-*` skills are for greenfield (no existing docs).\n`reconcile-*` skills are for repos that already have docs and need a refresh — they\ndetect drift between declared and actual state, propose updates, and (with `--apply`)\napply them. Both refuse to run in the wrong scenario.\n\n**Product registry ownership:** The central `product-registry.yaml` is the\ncanonical product taxonomy for the organization, **owned by Enterprise\nArchitects**. L3 and L2 owners declare their `product_id` from their own best\nunderstanding when scaffolding; the scaffold and reconcile skills do **not**\nconsult the registry. EAs reconcile registry drift centrally (taxonomy\nreview, new-product approval, retirement) using `/register-product` from\ninside the Level 1 working tree.\n\n## The 3-Level Model\n\n| Level | Owner | Scope | Key Files |\n|-------|-------|-------|-----------|\n| Level 1 | Enterprise Architects | Company-wide policy | [`architecture-cookbook`](https://github.com/PlayStudios-Architecture/architecture-cookbook) repo: `standards/`, schemas, company ADRs |\n| Level 2 | System Architects, Tech Directors | Product topology | `product.yaml`, `architecture.dsl`, `service-catalogue.md`, `flows/`, `decisions/` |\n| Level 3 | System Architects, Tech Leads | Repo-local architecture | `architecture.yaml`, `docs/architecture/`, `docs/flows/`, `docs/decisions/`, `docs/contracts/` |\n\n## Required Paths\n\n### Level 3 (Engineering Repos)\n- `/architecture.yaml` — Mandatory metadata\n- `/docs/architecture/overview.md` — Mandatory summary of purpose, boundaries, ownership\n- `/docs/architecture/components.md` — Internal component view (Core for high-value archetypes — `game-service`, `shared-platform`, `support-web`, `data-store` — at `standard_version` ≥ 2.1.0; Recommended otherwise)\n- `/docs/flows/` — Mandatory (at minimum a README explaining why empty)\n- `/docs/decisions/` — Mandatory local ADR directory\n- `/docs/contracts/` — Mandatory minimum viable contract\n- `/CLAUDE.md` — Recommended repo-root agent instruction file that points LLM agents to `architecture.yaml` and `docs/architecture/`\n- `/AGENTS.md` — Recommended repo-root pointer file that links to `CLAUDE.md` for tools honoring the `AGENTS.md` convention\n\n### Level 2 (Product Hubs)\n- `/product.yaml` — Mandatory product metadata\n- `/architecture.dsl` — Mandatory Structurizr DSL product map\n- `/landscape/` — Generated Structurizr outputs (auto-populated, not manual)\n- `/README.md` — Mandatory product overview\n- `/service-catalogue.md` — Mandatory logical-to-physical repo map\n- `/flows/` — Mandatory directory for cross-artifact product flows\n- `/decisions/` — Mandatory product-level ADR directory\n- `/CLAUDE.md` — Recommended hub-root agent instruction file that points LLM agents to `product.yaml` and `service-catalogue.md`\n- `/AGENTS.md` — Recommended hub-root pointer file that links to `CLAUDE.md`\n\n## Key Rules\n\n### Identifiers\n- `artifact_id` and `product_id` must be **stable kebab-case**: `^[a-z0-9]+(?:-[a-z0-9]+)*$`\n- ADR files: `ADR-NNN-short-title.md`\n- Flow files: kebab-case describing the journey\n\n### Archetypes (pick one per repo)\n- **`game-client`** — Player-facing runtime (Unity, Unreal, native mobile); usually no authoritative entities\n- **`game-service`** — Game-specific backend logic and source-of-truth state\n- **`shared-platform`** — Multi-tenant capabilities shared across games/products\n- **`sdk-library`** — Reusable integration code with stable public entry points\n- **`tool-chain`** — Engineering automation, CI helpers, build tooling, developer CLI\n- **`support-web`** — Backoffice portals, support tooling, launchers\n- **`data-store`** — Repos that *are* a data store: DB schema repos, SPROC/migration projects, dbt / warehouse modeling. Deployed by DB tooling (DACPAC, Flyway, Liquibase, Alembic, dbt), not as a runtime service\n\n### Contracts (by archetype)\n- **`game-service` / `shared-platform`:** Link to OpenAPI, gRPC, or endpoint summary + TLS config (ADR-002)\n- **`game-client`:** Document deep links, external triggers, integration entry points\n- **`sdk-library`:** Public entry points, lifecycle hooks, known side effects + TLS config (ADR-002)\n- **`tool-chain` / `support-web`:** Document CLI commands, automation entry points, operational boundaries\n- **`data-store`:** Public schemas / stored objects intended for callers, migration / deployment contract (versioning, breaking-change policy, idempotency), read/write authority split\n- TLS Configuration (ADR-002) is only required for `game-service`, `shared-platform`, `sdk-library`. The other archetypes (`game-client`, `tool-chain`, `support-web`, `data-store`) are exempt — they do not open service-to-service sockets themselves.\n\n### Data Ownership\n- **`authoritative`** — Source of truth for the entity\n- **`transient-cache`** — Temporary local copy that must eventually synchronize\n- **`replica`** — Read-only mirrored copy\n- **`consumer`** — Uses data without meaningful local copy\n\n### Lifecycle vs Version Status (independent fields)\n- **`lifecycle`** (L3 only, required): `discovery` | `active` | `maintenance` | `sunset`\n- **`version_status`** (L2 & L3, optional): `current` | `legacy` | `deprecated`\n- These are independent. A `legacy` artifact can be `active`. A `deprecated` artifact can be `maintenance`.\n\n### Compliance Tiers\n- **Core** — Minimum for LLM-readiness. Required paths exist, YAML validates against schema.\n- **Recommended** — Adds components.md, provides/consumes fields, shared_infrastructure, risks section, last_reviewed.\n- **Advanced** — CI validates schemas, checks paths, verifies cross-references.\n\n### Flow Scoping\n- Level 2 `flows/` — Cross-artifact flows, player journeys, flows spanning multiple services\n- Level 3 `docs/flows/` — Repo-internal implementation flows within a single artifact\n- Level 2 flows may link to Level 3 flows for deeper implementation detail\n\n### Authority & Conflict Resolution\n- Level 1: company-wide policy, reserved paths, identifier conventions\n- Level 2: product boundaries, membership, cross-service flows, shared contract shape\n- Level 3: repo internals, local flows, local technical standards, rollout timing\n- Temporary mismatches: document with owner and review date\n\n### ADR Scoping\n- **Company-wide** ([`standards/decisions/`](https://github.com/PlayStudios-Architecture/architecture-cookbook/tree/main/standards/decisions) in the L1 architecture-cookbook repo): Constraints applied uniformly across all products\n- **Product-level** (`decisions/` in L2 hub): Decisions affecting integration of all artifacts in a product\n- **Repo-local** (`docs/decisions/` in L3 repo): Decisions scoped to a single repo\n\n### Monorepo Note\nA monorepo may contain both Level 2 and Level 3 artifacts. Git boundary does not change architectural responsibility. Product-wide artifacts remain Level 2; repo-internal architecture remains Level 3.\n\n## Schema Reference\n\nLevel 3 `architecture.yaml` required fields:\n```\nstandard_version, compliance_tier,\nidentity: { artifact_id, artifact_name, product_id, domain, lifecycle },\nrepository: { structure, archetype, tenancy, url, owner, primary_language, framework, runtime }\n```\n\nLevel 2 `product.yaml` required fields:\n```\nstandard_version,\nproduct: { id, name, status, business_owner, technical_owner, summary },\nrepositories: { service_catalogue, landscape_dsl, decision_directory, flow_directory }\n```\n\n## Full Standard Reference\n\nThe long-form architecture standard lives in the Level 1 architecture-cookbook\nrepository (`PlayStudios-Architecture/architecture-cookbook`). This skill set\ndistills the operational rules; for the authoritative source consult the\ndocuments below. There is no requirement to clone L1 — these are reference\nlinks for human readers.\n\n- [`core-standard.md`](https://github.com/PlayStudios-Architecture/architecture-cookbook/blob/main/standards/core-standard.md) — All mandatory rules\n- [`compliance-tiers.md`](https://github.com/PlayStudios-Architecture/architecture-cookbook/blob/main/standards/compliance-tiers.md) — Tier requirements\n- [`guidance.md`](https://github.com/PlayStudios-Architecture/architecture-cookbook/blob/main/standards/guidance.md) — Archetypes, data ownership, glossary\n- [`schemas/level-3-architecture.yaml`](https://github.com/PlayStudios-Architecture/architecture-cookbook/blob/main/standards/schemas/level-3-architecture.yaml) — Level 3 schema\n- [`schemas/level-2-product.yaml`](https://github.com/PlayStudios-Architecture/architecture-cookbook/blob/main/standards/schemas/level-2-product.yaml) — Level 2 schema\n- [`standards/decisions/`](https://github.com/PlayStudios-Architecture/architecture-cookbook/tree/main/standards/decisions) — Company-wide ADRs\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
